{"kind":"artifact","text":"The worktrees response carries a remote only after the service has judged it linkable and repository-local. Exact clean pushed head 7da8b753682d38aa118e709943df0ee8c03e5c37; tree 29ac34a8, checkout clean, origin matching. Under planner's child 48ec44db and my promise ca8d07ed.\n\nProvenance is the only thing that changed since 512f5c5f: the tree is byte-identical at 29ac34a8, and the commit was amended so its Rests-On names the child rather than the parent. Codex refused the previous delivery at e0b657b8 and was right.\n\nWHAT CHANGED HERE. The transport shape is unchanged — remote, omitempty, from local.Remote — but what can reach it is not. TestWorktreesResponseIgnoresOutOfRepositoryRemoteConfiguration asserts against the live response body that an injected outer-scope origin cannot reach a client, and TestWorktreesResponseCarriesOnlyALinkableRemote covers future+git:// and x-unheard-of://, which parse with a host and so cannot be refused by the host check alone. That test belongs here rather than only in internal/app because this is where a value becomes something a browser receives, and the client is the layer most likely to be an older build.\n\nGates run by me at this exact head from a clean run I watched exit: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached; npm test --prefix ui 98/98; gofmt empty; build and vet exit 0; make ui leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"7da8b753682d38aa118e709943df0ee8c03e5c37","path":"internal/service"}}